"Positivity of Schubert coefficients (https://comptes-rendus.academie-sciences.fr/mathematique/item/10.5802/crmath.797.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; 2025, Vol. 363, p. ... # Positivity of Schubert coefficients ... ** The first author received support from the NSF grant CCF-2007891.\nACADÉMIE DES SCIENCES — INSTITUT DE FRANCE\n\nComptes Rendus. Mathématique  \n2025, Vol. 363, p. 1507-1515  \nhttps://doi.org/10.5802/crmath.797\n\nResearch article  \nAlgebra\n\n# Positivity of Schubert coefficients\n\nIgor Pak^a and Colleen Robichaux^a\n\n^a Department of Mathematics, UCLA, Los Angeles, CA 90095, USA  \nE-mails: pak@math.ucla.edu, robichaux@math.ucla.edu\n\n**Abstract.** Schubert coefficients c^w_{u,v} are structure constants describing multiplication of Schubert polynomials. Deciding positivity of Schubert coefficients is a major open problem in Algebraic Combinatorics. We prove a positive rule for this problem based on two well known assumptions: the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis and the strong derandomization assumption by Miltersen–Vinodchandran.\n\n**Keywords.** Schubert polynomials, Schubert structure constants, generalized Riemann hypothesis, derandomization, computational complexity, Hilbert Nullstellensatz, combinatorial interpretation.\n\n**2020 Mathematics Subject Classification.** 05E14.\n\n**Funding.** The first author received support from the NSF grant CCF-2007891.  \nThe second author received support from the NSF grant DMS-2302279.\n\n*Manuscript received 19 January 2025, revised and accepted 20 September 2025.*\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nIn the era of specialization we live in, it is common for experts in one area of mathematics to be unable to understand and evaluate results in another area. Occasionally, things turn for the worse, when one is unable to understand the *definitions*, or even the *notation*. The results then become expressions in a foreign language that take time, effort and dedication to translate.\n\nMost recently, we found ourselves in an unenviable position of having made progress on a major open problem in Algebraic Combinatorics [28], yet our results are stated in the language of Computational Complexity that was deep and technical enough to be inaccessible to most people interested in the problem. Given a choice of being understood but unmotivated vs. being motivated and misunderstood, we chose the former.\n\nThe main goal of our study is to give a *combinatorial interpretation for Schubert coefficients*, arguably the most important open problem in Schubert calculus. In [28], we analyze the *Schubert positivity problem*, traditionally a stepping stone towards a combinatorial interpretation and a major open problem in its own right. The main result in [28] is an upper bound for the complexity class of the Schubert positivity problem. We presented the results in a conventional CS theory style, while the proofs used some technical algebro-geometric arguments.\n\nIn this note, we address the Algebraic Combinatorics audience, giving implications of our main result in [28] using conventional combinatorics language. More precisely, we show that the Schubert positivity problem has a *positive rule* assuming two well known hypotheses. To streamline the presentation, we omit most of the Schubert calculus background that was included in [28], as well as the extensive complexity background which can be found, e.g., in [12,39].\n\nISSN (electronic): 1778-3569  \nhttps://comptes-rendus.academie-sciences.fr/mathematique/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPositivity conjecture for Newton-polytope support coefficients | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/366097/positivity-conjecture-for-newton-polytope-support-coefficien)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Support-coefficient positivity conjecture. ... Guo and Zhuowei Lin, “Schubert polynomials and patterns in permutations”, arXiv:2412.02932 (2024).\n\n# Positivity conjecture for Newton-polytope support coefficients\n\nAbout 2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nFor $w\\in S_n$w∈S n​, write\n\n$$\\theta_w=1+\\sum_u d_u\\,p_u(w),$$θ w​=1+u∑​d u​p u​(w),\n\nwhere $\\theta_w$θ w​ is the number of supports of $\\mathfrak{S}_w(x)$S w​(x) and $p_u(w)$p u​(w) counts occurrences of the permutation pattern $u$u in $w$w. The coefficients are computed recursively by\n\n$$d_u=\\theta_u-1-\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_\\ell\\atop \\ell<m}d_\\sigma\\,p_\\sigma(u),$$d u​=θ u​−1−ℓ<m σ∈S ℓ​​∑​d σ​p σ​(u),\n\nwith the same stability property $d_u=0$d u​=0 when the final entry of $u\\in S_m$u∈S m​ is $m$m. Support-coefficient positivity conjecture. For any permutation $u$u, $d_u\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{\\geq 0}$d u​∈Z≥0​. This is presented as an analogue of Gao's conjecture; no general resolution is given.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nPeter L. Guo and Zhuowei Lin, “Schubert polynomials and patterns in permutations”, arXiv:2412.02932 (2024).\n\nnewton-polytopespermutation-patternsschubert-polynomials\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch (Igor Pak Home Page) (https://www.math.ucla.edu/~pak/papers/research.htm)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; 13 (2025), Paper No. e162, 16 pp. ... We combine this with Purbhoo's algebraic criterion (2006) to derive the result. ...   * (with Colleen Robichaux) Positivity of Schubert Coefficients, Comptes Rendus Acad.\nWe combine this with Purbhoo's algebraic criterion (2006) to derive the result.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the arXiv version of the paper.\nDownload .pdf file of the journal version of the paper.\nSee also online version on the journal's website.\n\nNote: Journal version has somewhat stronger results, more general statements and completely different, more streamlined proofs. However, this version is missing appendices and some CS background/applications.\n\n  * (with Colleen Robichaux) Positivity of Schubert Coefficients, Comptes Rendus Acad. Sci. Paris, Ser. I. Math., vol. 363 (2025), 1507�1515.\n\nSchubert coefficients are structure constants describing multiplication of Schubert polynomials. Deciding positivity of Schubert coefficients is a major open problem in Algebraic Combinatorics. We prove a positive rule for this problem based on two assumptions: Generalized Riemann hypothesis (GRH) and Miltersen–Vinodchandran's Assumption (MVA).\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\nSee my blog post on the paper.\nSee also video of Colleen's talk at the Fields Institute on the paper.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSchubert polynomials | SymCat (https://www.symmetricfunctions.com/schubert.htm)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; This problem is closely related to the Fomin–Kirillov conjecture, see [FK99, Conjecture 8.1 and Problem 8.3]. ... 6.4 and Prop. ... Lin give lower bounds for the number of support monomials of a Schubert polynomial, equivalently lattice points in its Newton polytope, in terms of permutation-pattern occurrences [GL24]. ... They also formulate a conditional positive rule for deciding Schubert coefficient positivity [PR24a].\nP. L. Guo and Z. Lin give lower bounds for the number of support monomials of a Schubert polynomial, equivalently lattice points in its Newton polytope, in terms of permutation-pattern occurrences [GL24]. Their framework is the more general setting of dual characters of flagged Weyl modules.\n\n### #Complexity\n\nDeciding whether a monomial coefficient, or a Schubert structure constant coefficient \\(c^w_{uv},\\) is nonzero can be done in probabilistic polynomial time; see [ARY21, PR25b]. Avery St. Dizier and A. Yong connect generalized permutahedra with Schubert calculus [DY20]. They obtain sufficient vanishing criteria for Schubert intersection numbers, using Schubitopes as Newton polytopes of Schubert polynomials, and give a polynomial-time tableau test. I. Pak and C. Robichaux prove that the vanishing problem for Schubert coefficients lies in \\(\\mathrm{coAM}\\) assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis [PR24b]. They also formulate a conditional positive rule for deciding Schubert coefficient positivity [PR24a]. I. Pak and C. Robichaux also give a signed puzzle rule for Schubert coefficients based on Knutson’s recurrence [PR25a]. They use it to prove polynomiality for sums of Schubert coefficients with bounded number of inversions.\n\nFor type B/C/D, skew, double, affine, involution, quantum, universal, and twisted variants, see the companion page on Schubert polynomial variations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVanishing of Schubert coefficients via the effective Hilbert nullstellensatz (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-sigma/article/vanishing-of-schubert-coefficients-via-the-effective-hilbert-nullstellensatz/D77AAE02190D78722AB74667FF85AA85)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Financial support ... © The Author(s), 2025. ... Additionally, positivity of Schubert coefficients is the main assumption in the generalized Horn inequalities, see, for example, [Reference Anderson, Richmond and Yong4, Reference Berenstein and Sjamaar7, Reference Berline, Vergne and Walter8].\n\n# Vanishing of Schubert coefficients via the effective Hilbert nullstellensatz\n\nPart of: Special varieties Theory of computing Projective and enumerative geometry\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  01 October 2025\n\nIgor Pak [Opens in a new window]  and\n\nColleen Robichaux [Opens in a new window]\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nIgor Pak\n    Affiliation:\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, 90095 CA, USA; E-mail: pak@math.ucla.edu\n\nColleen Robichaux*\n    Affiliation:\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, University of California , Los Angeles, Los Angeles, 90095 CA, USA\n\n*\n    \n\nE-mail: robichaux@math.ucla.edu (Corresponding author)\n\n* * *\n\nArticle contents\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Introduction\n  * Definitions and notation\n  * Proof of Main Lemma\n  * Final remarks\n  * Competing interest\n  * Financial support\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSINGULAR CHERN CLASSES OF SCHUBERT (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=d491c14e769763e778d0860a07c6449f36094f9c&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; tions of the CSM classes of all Schubert cells in Gr(3, C6) using this method. ... Then the coeﬃcient of ... Theorem 5.8 can be seen as further support for the truth of the full positivity conjecture.\nSINGULAR CHERN CLASSES OF SCHUBERT\nVARIETIES VIA SMALL RESOLUTION\n3\nIn section 4 we discuss the pushforward of the class cSM(Z) to the particular Grass-\nmannian which contains the Schubert variety being resolved. This computes the main\ningredient of (1-1).\nWe give two methods for computing the pushforward.\nThe ﬁrst\nmethod uses the localization theorem for equivariant Chow groups. We present calcula-\ntions of the CSM classes of all Schubert cells in Gr(3, C6) using this method. The second\nmethod is based on an explicit description of the Gysin map for Grassmannian bundles\ngiven by Fulton and Pragacz.\nThe second main result is Theorem 5.8 where we verify a new case of the positivity\nconjecture from [1].\nTheorem. Let Xβ be a codimension 1 Schubert variety of Xα. Then the coeﬃcient of\n[Xβ] in cSM(X◦\nα) is non-negative.\nWe remark at the end of § 5.2 that codimension 1 coeﬃcients are in fact strictly positive\nand we give a formula for them. Empirical evidence suggests that codimension 1 coeﬃ-\ncients are the smallest among all coeﬃcients which may a priori appear. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch (Igor Pak Home Page) (https://math.ucla.edu/~pak/papers/research.htm)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday;   * (with Zachary Slonim) Stretched Schubert coefficients are eventually quasi-polynomial, preprint (2026), 25 pp. ... As a consequence of the proof, we also present new counterexamples to the saturation conjecture for Schubert coefficients, and give computational applications. ... 9 (2026), 1479�1495\n## Algebraic Combinatorics and Young tableaux\n\n  * (with Zachary Slonim) Stretched Schubert coefficients are eventually quasi-polynomial, preprint (2026), 25 pp.\n\nFor a permutation u in S_{n}, let N∗u be the permutation in S_{Nn} with scaled Lehmer's code. For given u,v,w in S_{n} and integer N, the stretched Schubert coefficients are defined as f(N):=c(N∗u,N∗v,N∗w). Our main result is that the function f(N) is eventually quasi-polynomial. This proves Kirillov's conjecture [Kir04, Conj. 6.28], that the generating function for the sequence {f(N)} is rational.\n\nFor the proof, we use combinatorics of pipe dreams to show that Schubert coefficients are given as an alternating sum of the numbers of integer points in certain polytopes. These polytopes behave nicely under stretching, and we use Ehrhart theory to obtain the result. As a consequence of the proof, we also present new counterexamples to the saturation conjecture for Schubert coefficients, and give computational applications.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\n  * (with Colleen Robichaux) Saturation property fails for Schubert coefficients, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. vol. 155 (2027), no. 1, 73�85.\n\nSaturation property for the Littlewood�Richardson coefficients was established by Knutson and Tao in 1999. In 2004, Kirillov conjectured that the saturation property extends to Schubert coefficients. We disprove this conjecture in a strong form, by showing that it fails for a large family of instances. We also refute the saturation property for Schubert coefficients under bit scaling, and discuss computational complexity implications.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\n* * *\n\n  * (with Greta Panova and Joshua P. Swanson) A combinatorial interpretation for certain plethysm and Kronecker coefficients, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc., vol. 155 (2027), no. 1, 57�71.\n\nWe give explicit positive combinatorial interpretations for certain plethysm and Kronecker coefficients.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\n* * *\n\n  * (with Colleen Robichaux and Weihong Xu) On vanishing of Gromov–Witten invariants, preprint (2025), 11 pp.\n\nWe consider the decision problem whether a particular Gromov–Witten invariant on a partial flag variety is zero. We prove that for the 3-pointed, genus zero invariants, this problem is in the complexity class AM assuming the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH), and therefore lies in the second level of polynomial hierarchy PH.\n\nFor the proof, we construct an explicit system of polynomial equations through a translation of the defining equations. We also obtain an extension of the Parametric Hilbert's Nullstellensatz to prove our central reduction.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\n  * (with Colleen Robichaux) Vanishing of Schubert coefficients in probabilistic polynomial time, preprint (2025), 15 pp.\n\nThe Schubert vanishing problem asks whether Schubert structure constants are zero. We give a complete solution of the problem from an algorithmic point of view, by showing that Schubert vanishing can be decided in probabilistic polynomial time.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\nSee video and slides of my talk at ICERM.\nSee video and slides of my talk at WAC'26.\nSee also Colleen's talk at NDU.\n\n  * (with Colleen Robichaux) Signed puzzles for Schubert coefficients, Algebraic Combinatorics, vol. 9 (2026), 1479�1495\n\nWe give a signed puzzle rule to compute Schubert coefficients. The rule is based on a careful analysis of Knutson's recurrence [Knu03]. We use the rule to prove polynomiality of the sums of Schubert coefficients with bounded number of inversions.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\nNote: For n large enough, an alternative proof was given by Ada Seltzer in this paper. The author is non-specific whether their proof resolves questions in §9.2, but that seems likely true.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe higher-genus dual conjecture for Laplacian quadratic forms — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/328852/the-higher-genus-dual-conjecture-for-laplacian-quadratic-for)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The higher-genus dual conjecture for Laplacian quadratic forms ... An Huang, Shing-Tung Yau and Mei-Heng Yueh, “Graph invariants from ideas in physics and number theory”, arXiv:1409.5853 (2015).\n\nThe higher-genus dual conjecture for Laplacian quadratic forms\n\nLet $G_1$G 1​ and $G_2$G 2​ be connected graphs of the same genus $g$g, and let $\\Sigma_g$Σ g​ be the genus-$g$g surface. Suppose that $G_1$G 1​ and $G_2$G 2​ have embeddings into $\\Sigma_g$Σ g​ such that their corresponding dual graphs are isomorphic as multigraphs.\n\nHigher-genus dual conjecture. The quadratic forms over $\\mathbb{Z}$Z represented by the combinatorial Laplacians of $G_1$G 1​ and $G_2$G 2​ are equivalent.\n\nThis extends the proposed planar statement to embeddings in minimal-genus surfaces. The source presents it as an extension of the planar conjecture and gives no evidence of a resolution.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nAn Huang, Shing-Tung Yau and Mei-Heng Yueh, 